                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>St Clair suggests that the diary records two visits in January 1815 from George Cannon, a friend of <persName key="EAT01">Daniel Isaac Eaton</persName> involved
    in 'the murky dangerous world of illegal publishing'. He wrote a number of public letters on
    religious persecution under the pseudonym Erasmus Perkins, and edited a magazine, <title>The Theological Advisor</title>, that was
    heavily indebted to <persName key="SHE01">Percy Shelley</persName> for inspiration. </p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="printedbook">St Clair, p. 394.</bibl>
                    <bibl type="printedbook"><title>The journals of Mary Shelley, 1814–1844</title>, ed. by Paula R. Feldman and Diana Scott-Kilvert, 2 vols (Oxford 
    and New York: Oxford University Press; Clarendon Press, 1987), p. 62.</bibl>
